Z||000000||94ZonCrack||11-13-2001||04:22 PM||||I have a question concerning the belt used on BubbaJoe's Caddy V8 swaps. I have seen a few different belts used. I don't know what to get? Is it from another GM car / make / model.<P>This is the setup I plan to use ...<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/36bc6a50.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/36bc6a50.jpg</A> <P>Then there is also this with AC. I will probably not use this one but still would like to know what belt is used.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/402d15a0.jpg" TARGET=_blank>http://members.fortunecity.com/bubbajoexx/402d15a0.jpg</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||rockcrawl||11-13-2001||05:21 PM||||I suggest putting an idler where the A/C compressor is, or leaving the compressor there even if you won't use it. Without it there may not be enough belt surface on the water pump pulley. The coolant in a Fiero has a long way to travel, you need all the grip you can get on the w/p pulley. You will never see a factory belt routing using only 1/4 of the pulley. You need to use a piece of string to go around the pullies and then measure the total length of the string. I actually use an old broken Caddy belt for this. You need to learn the numbering system for the brand of belt you want to use, or find a parts store where the employees know there job. A 6 rib 76" belt will be a Kelley Springfield 760K6. A 56.2" 5 rib is 562K5. If you're lucky there will be something available that's close enough. You may need to go to a 5 rib belt to get the correct length.<p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 11-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||94ZonCrack||11-13-2001||05:42 PM||||Thanks thats exactly the kind of info I needed to know!!!||||reg||1
